  "notes": [
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "axes.state",
      "message": "Focus takes the field's :focus-within rule (InputStack.tsx L43-L46): the field shows focus while its input has it. Interactable's :focus-visible outline (Interactable.tsx L80-L85) never draws, since the field is a span that takes no focus. While focused, TextInput also sets focusedVariant (TextInput.tsx L218-L219), which tints an icon in the start and end slots through TextInputFocusVariantContext (InputIcon.tsx L36-L43) and the caret; a forced :focus-within sets no React state, so the Focus variants keep the icons in fg."
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "axes.state",
      "message": "Active takes Interactable's :active rule (Interactable.tsx L57-L64), the pressed background and child opacity. In use, pressing the field also focuses its input, so Active is drawn without the focus border, as the State is forced on its own."
    },
    {
      "level": "warning",
      "at": "paint.Field.fill",
      "message": "Interactable blends the field's background for each state with getBlendedColor (Interactable.tsx L289, L301 and L313), at opacityHovered, opacityPressed and opacityDisabled (interactable.ts L1-L4). Those colours are Synthesized tokens (ADR 0009), which the Foundations document makes only from a family's cds-common token map (#396), and cds-common has no tokens/textinput.js. So the Hover, Active and Disabled fields keep the enabled fill, bg or bgSecondary, and the render comparison lists the difference: in Light, a hovered field is rgb(247, 247, 247) on the page and #ffffff in the Contract."
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "layout[state=focus].Field.strokeWeight",
      "message": "A focused field has a 1px border and a 1px box-shadow ring outside it, both in the focus colour (InputStack.tsx L43-L46, with --border-width-focused at L253). The Contract draws them as one 2px centred stroke, which covers the same pixels. The render comparison reads the border alone, so it lists each Focus field as borderWidth page 1, Contract 2."
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "layout.Field.strokeAlign",
      "message": "CSS lays the field's children inside its 1px border (borderWidth, InputStack.tsx L285). A Figma inside stroke takes no layout space, so in Figma the field is 2px shorter than CDS's and its children reach under the border by 1px."
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "axes.start",
      "message": "A start node moves the input's start padding to space-0_5 (data-start, TextInput.tsx L64-L67), and the stacked label's too (insideLabelCssStartCss, L77-L79). So the start icon is a Slot axis (ADR 0011), not a BOOLEAN. The end node moves nothing else, so the end icon keeps its BOOLEAN."
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "anatomy.Start",
      "message": "An inside label sits inline at sizes s and m, in the start node before the start content (insideHorizontalLabel, TextInput.tsx L441-L467), so Start holds the Inline label and the Start icon. At size l it stacks above the input inside the field (insideVerticalLabel, L281-L283; InputStack.tsx L297-L301), so Input stack holds the Stacked label and the Input."
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "properties.swapStartIcon",
      "message": "start and end take any React node (TextInput.tsx L146-L149). The Contract models an InputIcon holding an icon name, as the docs place one (_webExamples.mdx L333, L369 and L418), and the preview renders that. A pressable InputIconButton (L250 and L263), a suffix (TextInput.tsx L375-L379) or other content goes in by hand."
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "anatomy.Error icon",
      "message": "A negative helper text starts with an info icon (HelperText.tsx L59-L72), the active (filled) glyph. The icon source has the inactive glyphs only, so the Error icon shows info's outline glyph. Its marginTop lowers it to the middle of the first line (L51); the Helper row centres it, which places it the same for one line."
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "family",
      "message": "readOnly is not an axis: it fills the input and the start and end nodes with bgSecondary inside the variant's border (TextInput.tsx L262-L267; NativeInput.tsx L40-L42), and as an axis it would double the set to 720 variants. The owner can add it if read-only fields are drawn often. bordered={false} (TextInput.tsx L209 and L361), focusedBorderWidth, enableColorSurge, align, width, height, borderRadius, inputBackground, labelFont and labelColor customise one input, so they are not axes either. compact is the deprecated alias of size s (dropped from size)."
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "at": "code",
      "message": "CDS's own Code Connect file (TextInput.figma.ts) maps one state enum to variant positive or negative, readOnly or disabled (L13-L25), and leaves the other variants out. The Contract follows TextInput.tsx: the six variants of InputBaseProps.ts and the canonical State axis."
    }
  ],
